<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-descriptive-statistics_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Descriptive Statistics: Kenyan Rift Valley Flamingos</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Between 2021 and 2023, wildlife researchers measured 344 flamingos across three 
      saline lakes in the Kenyan Rift Valley: Lake Nakuru, Lake Bogoria, and Lake Elementaita. 
      The dataset records two distinct species—the Lesser Flamingo (<init>Phoeniconaias minor</init>) 
      and the Greater Flamingo (<init>Phoeniconopterus roseus</init>)—along with morphometric 
      measurements including body mass (g), bill length (mm), bill depth (mm), and leg length (mm).
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter, we use this dataset to learn how to visualize, summarize, and compare 
      distributions of real-world biological data.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 6.1: SOFTWARE-AGNOSTIC DATA VISUALIZATION                    -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="sec-visualizing-flamingos">
    <title>Visualizing Distributions in the Field</title>

    <p>
      When examining raw data from the field, tabular numbers can hide overall patterns. 
      Consider the body mass of all 336 recorded flamingos in our sample, which spans 
      from 1206.0 g to 3982.0 g. To understand how body mass is distributed across the 
      population, we construct a frequency histogram.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-mass-histogram">
      <caption>Distribution of body mass (g) for 336 flamingos in Lake Nakuru, Bogoria, and Elementaita.</caption>
      <image source="images/flamingo_mass_histogram.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>A bimodal histogram showing two distinct clusters of body mass around 1650g and 3100g.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Notice that the histogram in <xref ref="fig-flamingo-mass-histogram"/> is not single-peaked. 
      Instead, it is <term>bimodal</term>, exhibiting a major peak around 1650 g and a secondary, 
      smaller peak near 3100 g. In ecology, multimodal distributions often signal the presence 
      of distinct sub-populations mixed together.
    </p>

    <p>
      When we split the data by species, the cause of the double peak becomes immediate: 
      Lesser Flamingos (<m>n = 273</m>) form a symmetric cluster centered around 1689.1 g, 
      whereas Greater Flamingos (<m>n = 63</m>) form a separate cluster centered around 3120.2 g.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-species-boxplot">
      <caption>Comparative boxplots of bill length (mm) by species across Kenyan sampling sites.</caption>
      <image source="images/flamingo_bill_boxplot.png" width="75%">
        <shortdescription>Side-by-side boxplots comparing bill length between Greater Flamingos and Lesser Flamingos.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 6.2: SOFTWARE-AGNOSTIC CENTER AND SPREAD                     -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="sec-center-and-spread">
    <title>Measuring Center and Spread</title>

    <p>
      To summarize a numerical distribution, we require measures of central tendency and variation.
      For a sample of measurements <m>x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n</m>, the <term>sample mean</term> <m>\bar{x}</m> is calculated as:
    </p>

    <me>
      \bar{x} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i
    </me>

    <p>
      Across all 344 bill length observations, the pooled mean bill length is 103.5 mm, with a sample 
      standard deviation of <m>s = 12.4</m> mm. However, because our dataset combines two distinct species, 
      the overall median is 99.1 mm and the interquartile range (<m>\text{IQR} = Q_3 - Q_1</m>) is 8.1 mm 
      (<m>Q_1 = 95.9</m> mm, <m>Q_3 = 104.0</m> mm).
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-flamingo-summary-stats">
      <caption>Summary statistics for body mass (g) and bill length (mm) by species.</caption>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Species</cell>
          <cell>Variable</cell>
          <cell>Count (<m>n</m>)</cell>
          <cell>Mean (<m>\bar{x}</m>)</cell>
          <cell>Std Dev (<m>s</m>)</cell>
          <cell>Median</cell>
          <cell>IQR</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Body Mass (g)</cell>
          <cell>273</cell>
          <cell>1689.1</cell>
          <cell>196.4</cell>
          <cell>1684.0</cell>
          <cell>307.0</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Bill Length (mm)</cell>
          <cell>278</cell>
          <cell>97.9</cell>
          <cell>4.2</cell>
          <cell>97.7</cell>
          <cell>5.7</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Greater Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Body Mass (g)</cell>
          <cell>63</cell>
          <cell>3120.2</cell>
          <cell>313.9</cell>
          <cell>3102.0</cell>
          <cell>326.5</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Greater Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Bill Length (mm)</cell>
          <cell>66</cell>
          <cell>127.1</cell>
          <cell>6.1</cell>
          <cell>127.3</cell>
          <cell>9.5</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Notice in <xref ref="table-flamingo-summary-stats"/> that within each single species group, 
      the mean and median are nearly identical (e.g., 1689.1 g mean vs. 1684.0 g median for Lesser Flamingos). 
      This indicates that within a single species, body mass follows a symmetric distribution.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 6.3: RELATIONSHIPS BETWEEN VARIABLES                         -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="sec-relationships">
    <title>Relationships Between Categorical and Numerical Traits</title>

    <p>
      Ecologists often study how species are distributed across different habitat sites. 
      <xref ref="table-lake-species-crosstab"/> summarizes the counts of flamingos observed at each lake.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-lake-species-crosstab">
      <caption>Contingency table of flamingo species counts by Rift Valley lake.</caption>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Lake</cell>
          <cell>Greater Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell>
          <cell>Total</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Lake Bogoria</cell>
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>114</cell>
          <cell>124</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Lake Elementaita</cell>
          <cell>30</cell>
          <cell>38</cell>
          <cell>68</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Lake Nakuru</cell>
          <cell>26</cell>
          <cell>126</cell>
          <cell>152</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Total</cell>
          <cell>66</cell>
          <cell>278</cell>
          <cell>344</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      From the contingency table, we calculate conditional proportions. For example, at Lake Bogoria, 
      <m>114 / 124 \approx 91.9\%</m> of observed birds are Lesser Flamingos, reflecting the massive 
      cyanobacteria blooms that attract filter-feeding Lesser Flamingos to Bogoria's alkaline waters.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 6.4: CONFINED R INVESTIGATION TUTORIAL                       -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <section xml:id="sec-investigation-r-flamingos">
    <title>Investigation: Analyzing Flamingo Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In this investigation, you will use R to reproduce the numerical summaries and 
        visualizations presented in Sections 6.1 through 6.3 using the dataset 
        <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-r-loading-data">
      <title>Loading the Dataset and Exploring Structure</title>
      <p>
        Begin by loading the CSV file into your R workspace and inspecting its dimensions:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
# Load the dataset
flamingos &lt;- read.csv("kenya_flamingos.csv")

# View the structure and first few rows
str(flamingos)
head(flamingos)
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-r-summary-stats">
      <title>Computing Summary Statistics</title>
      <p>
        Calculate overall summary statistics, as well as grouped measures by species using 
        base R or the <c>dplyr</c> package:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
# Overall summary of body mass and bill length
summary(flamingos$body_mass_g)
mean(flamingos$bill_length_mm, na.rm = TRUE)
sd(flamingos$bill_length_mm, na.rm = TRUE)

# Grouped summary statistics using dplyr
library(dplyr)

flamingos %&gt;%
  group_by(species) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    count = n(),
    mean_mass = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
    sd_mass   = sd(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_mass = median(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
    iqr_mass  = IQR(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-r-visualizations">
      <title>Generating Visualizations</title>
      <p>
        Recreate the histograms and side-by-side boxplots using <c>ggplot2</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
library(ggplot2)

# Histogram of body mass
ggplot(flamingos, aes(x = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 100, fill = "firebrick", color = "white") +
  labs(
    title = "Distribution of Flamingo Body Mass",
    x = "Body Mass (g)",
    y = "Count"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

# Comparative boxplot of bill length by species
ggplot(flamingos, aes(x = species, y = bill_length_mm, fill = species)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  labs(
    title = "Bill Length Comparison by Species",
    x = "Species",
    y = "Bill Length (mm)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-r-contingency">
      <title>Contingency Tables</title>
      <p>
        Construct a two-way cross-tabulation of sampling lakes and species:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
# Frequency table of lake vs. species
lake_table &lt;- table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species)
addmargins(lake_table)

# Proportions by lake (row proportions)
prop.table(lake_table, margin = 1)
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-r-flamingos-investigation">
      <title>Investigation Exercises</title>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Filter the dataset to include only flamingos sampled at Lake Nakuru in 2022. 
            Calculate the mean and standard deviation of <c>leg_length_mm</c> for this subset.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Generate a scatterplot of <c>bill_length_mm</c> versus <c>body_mass_g</c> colored 
            by <c>species</c>. Describe the direction and strength of the association.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>

</chapter>
